# GeoFrame spike

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:geoframe, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
    {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
    {:maplibre, "~> 0.1.9"},
    {:kino_maplibre, "~> 0.1.13"}
  ]
)
```

## Introduction

GeoFrame is a GeoPandas-style geospatial dataframe for Elixir. Geometry lives in Rust as a
GeoArrow column, attributes live in an `Explorer.DataFrame`, and the two stay aligned through
every row operation.

This notebook walks through the Phase 0 spike: read a GeoParquet file, inspect it, compute
planar measures, filter rows, draw it, and write it back.

The map tab uses MapLibre's demo basemap, so your browser needs internet access.

## Read a GeoParquet file

The fixture holds four polygons and one point near Miami, in `EPSG:4326`, with a missing
`population` value to show how nulls travel.

```elixir
path = Path.join([__DIR__, "..", "test", "fixtures", "neighborhoods.parquet"]) |> Path.expand()
gdf = GeoFrame.read_parquet!(path)
```

The cell above ends in a `GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame`, which Livebook renders with **Map**,
**Table** and **Inspect** tabs.

Pieces are available separately:

```elixir
{GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.n_rows(gdf), GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.names(gdf),
 GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.crs(gdf)}
```

```elixir
GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data(gdf)
```

## Measures

Measures are planar, in the units of the CRS (here square degrees, which is fine for a demo
but not a real area). Each returns an `Explorer.Series`; the point has zero area and length.

```elixir
area = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.area(gdf)
perimeter = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.length(gdf)

{Explorer.Series.to_list(area), Explorer.Series.to_list(perimeter)}
```

```elixir
GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.bounds(gdf)
```

```elixir
GeoFrame.GeoSeries.total_bounds(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.geometry(gdf))
```

Centroids replace the geometry and keep the attributes aligned:

```elixir
GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.centroid(gdf)
```

## Reproject and measure in metres

Square degrees are not an area. `to_crs/2` reprojects (pure Rust, no PROJ install), and
`estimate_utm_crs/1` picks the UTM zone that fits the data. Axis order is always
x = longitude/easting, y = latitude/northing.

```elixir
utm = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.estimate_utm_crs(gdf)
metric = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs(gdf, utm)

{utm, GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.crs(metric)}
```

Areas are now square metres, and lengths metres:

```elixir
{Explorer.Series.to_list(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.area(metric)),
 Explorer.Series.to_list(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.length(metric))}
```

Without leaving degrees, `area_geodesic/1` measures on the WGS 84 ellipsoid directly, and
agrees with the UTM area to within a fraction of a percent (`length_geodesic/1` and
`distance(a, b, method: :geodesic)` do the same for lengths and distances):

```elixir
geodesic = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.geometry(gdf) |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.area_geodesic()
utm_area = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.area(metric)

{Explorer.Series.to_list(geodesic), Explorer.Series.to_list(utm_area)}
```

An integer EPSG code works too (here Web Mercator), and a series without a CRS raises, as in
GeoPandas, so call `set_crs/2` first:

```elixir
gdf |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs(3857) |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.crs()
```

## Filtering

`filter_with/2` takes the attribute columns, picks the rows, and applies the same row
positions to the geometry.

```elixir
residential =
  GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter_with(gdf, fn df ->
    Explorer.Series.equal(df["kind"], "residential")
  end)
```

Add the area as an attribute, then sort by it:

```elixir
gdf
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate_with(fn _df ->
  [area: GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.area(gdf)]
end)
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.sort_with(&[desc: &1["area"]])
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
```

## Maps

Rendering uses `GeoFrame.Kino.map/2` under the hood. Call it directly to get the map alone or
to pass options such as `max_features:` (inputs above the cap, 50,000 by default, show a
notice instead of a map):

```elixir
GeoFrame.Kino.map(gdf)
```

A series draws on its own too:

```elixir
GeoFrame.Kino.map(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.geometry(residential))
```

Export to GeoJSON for any other tool:

```elixir
GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_geojson(residential) |> Map.fetch!("features") |> hd()
```

## Z coordinates

Geometries can carry a Z ordinate (and M). `from_xy/3` takes `z:`, and `get_coordinates/2`
returns it with `include_z: true`. The map draws the 2D projection; Inspect shows the Z.

```elixir
peaks = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.from_xy([-80.19, -80.2], [25.77, 25.78], z: [12.5, 30.0], crs: "EPSG:4326")

{GeoFrame.GeoSeries.to_wkt(peaks), GeoFrame.GeoSeries.get_coordinates(peaks, include_z: true)}
```

```elixir
peaks
```

## Write and read back

```elixir
out = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "geoframe_spike_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}.parquet")
:ok = GeoFrame.write_parquet(residential, out)
back = GeoFrame.read_parquet!(out)

File.rm!(out)

{GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.n_rows(back), GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.crs(back)}
```
